[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.700]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from August 22nd, 2005 titled Introduction and One-Year Retrospective.
[00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:13.600]   I run a company that is defining a new category of data management software, known as an XML content server.
[00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:19.400]   I've decided to create this blog to record and communicate my experiences in watching this technology/market develop.
[00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:27.600]   It's a fascinating market to watch because XML content servers live at the intersection of several existing markets, DBMS, search, and content management.
[00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:32.400]   While I've been at MarcLogit for a year, I've just gotten around to starting this blog in August 2005.
[00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.700]   I'll summarize the past year quickly in this post.
[00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:40.900]   Here's what happened, XML content servers have continued to be successful in the publishing and government vertical markets.
[00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:47.100]   Major publishers are realizing that they should not be storing their content on the file system and accessing it with a search engine.
[00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:54.900]   They also know that today's various RDBMS extensions, for example blobs, clobs, XML shredding, do not work for their content.
[00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:59.900]   Thus, they are open to trying a new approach, and one that handles XML content natively.
[00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:05.600]   On the government front, the government has a predilection for XML because government loves open standards.
[00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:09.300]   In commercial markets, Todd. I people say, "Why XML?"
[00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.900]   In government, people say, "Why not XML?"
[00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:15.500]   It's open, it's not controlled by any vendor, it's self-documenting.
[00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:19.300]   In short, it's the perfect format for archiving and searching public content.
[00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:23.100]   XML content servers have started to make inroads in other markets.
[00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:27.400]   For example, aerospace, financial services, life sciences, legal,
[00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:31.500]   have all started to show interest in the category for applications such as flight manuals,
[00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:35.000]   financial publishing, research analysis, and eDiscovery.
[00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:38.700]   XML databases have separated into two subcategories.
[00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:45.100]   One group of XML database vendors has focused on managing persistent data on a middle tier for data integration applications,
[00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:48.800]   now known as EII applications, for example, Ipedo.
[00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:54.700]   Another smaller group has focused on managing XML content or documents, for example, MarkLogic.
[00:01:54.700 --> 00:02:00.200]   Personally, I think the first data-oriented strategy will lead those vendors to the exact same destination
[00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:03.700]   as the object database vendors, that is, nowhere.
[00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:07.900]   That's because the big relational database vendors will always fight hard to control data.
[00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:12.100]   On the flip side, the big relational vendors have always paid only lip service to content,
[00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:15.500]   which is helping to create the market for XML content servers.
[00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:21.300]   Industry analysts, consultants, and other pundits and gurus have suddenly seemed to change their collective view.
[00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:28.100]   For years, they simply said that virtually any new database problem can be handled by yet another extension to the relational model.
[00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:33.100]   For years, they cited the carnage in the ODBMS market as the example of how the relational vendors stomped
[00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:36.700]   an emerging category out of existence through signaling strategies,
[00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:40.300]   that is, wait for Oracle 8 and RDBMS extensions.
[00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:46.200]   For years, they seemed to overlook the great success of OLAP servers formerly called Multidimensional Database M,
[00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:52.800]   Anagement Systems, and the focused success of other special purpose, DBMS and Indexing Products, for example,
[00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.500]   Bitnap Indexing such as Sybase IQ.
[00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:00.700]   For example, in talking with one of the top database analysts at Gartner Donald Feinberg a few months back,
[00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:05.600]   I was both surprised and pleased to see that he was not apparently of the relational Uber alleys camp,
[00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:12.100]   but indeed questioned the use of the R and RDBMS, arguing that it's been so extended that it's not relational anymore,
[00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:17.900]   and indeed said he saw a market for special purpose, DBMSs for a variety of different applications.
[00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:22.400]   Another example would be a recent article by longtime database and search pundit Kurt Monash,
[00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:27.900]   where he argues similarly, across a number of areas including OLAP and documents.
[00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:32.100]   This audio version was created by Grant Duncan and AI, with permission from Dave.
