[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.400]   This is an audio version of a Dave Kellogg post from August 24, 2005 titled "Square Pegs and
[00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:11.360]   Round Holes." They say, "If your only tool is a hammer, then every problem looks like a nail."
[00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.840]   That's certainly a good way to think about how relational DBMS spenders have approached content.
[00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.880]   After all, if you're running a multi-billion-dollar database business,
[00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:22.400]   then you are going to have two built-in biases when thinking about content.
[00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:26.320]   When you will want to make content look like data and D2, you will assume the only thing
[00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:30.000]   people want to do with content is integrate it with content is integrate it with data.
[00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.920]   This posting addresses the first point, making content look like data.
[00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:39.120]   Another one we'll explore the second. If your world revolves around square tables,
[00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.960]   then it's not surprising that your reaction to the content problem is to find a way to squeeze
[00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:48.160]   documents into tables. The most obvious approach is to shove them into a column. That's exactly
[00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:53.520]   what the RDBMS vendors did, first with blobs and later with clubs. Using this approach,
[00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.680]   a document is placed into a table that looks like a bit of metadata plus the document itself,
[00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:01.760]   such as "bunch of docs, doc/id, author, title, date, document."
[00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:06.640]   Given that you can do virtually nothing with a blob, and fairly little with a club,
[00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:10.640]   most customers who want to do text searches will use an enterprise search engine to index
[00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:15.280]   the documents in the database. The search engine spider walks through the table and indexes the
[00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:19.600]   words in each document, instead of the search engine index saying that you can find documents
